  {
   "slug": "whats-in-season-november",
   "title": "What's in season in November",
   "date": "2026-10-17T09:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Leeks, parsnips, the last apples and the first sprouts. A guide to the late-autumn shelves.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>November is when the fields go quiet, but the store rooms are full. These are the fruit and vegetables you will see on our shelves and in the boxes this month.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Vegetables</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Leeks and parsnips</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Celeriac and swede</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Kale and cavolo nero</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The first Brussels sprouts</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Fruit</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Late apples: russet and Bramley</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Conference pears</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Quince, for jelly</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Stored plums, while they last</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Root vegetables keep for weeks in a cool, dark place. Leave the mud on; it protects them.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Squash keeps all winter on a shelf at room temperature, so buy an extra one now while the price is low and the flavour is at its best.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "seasonal"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "autumn"
   ],
   "featured_media": "fields",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "refill-shop-guide",
   "title": "A beginner's guide to our refill shelves",
   "date": "2026-10-05T10:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Bring a jar, weigh it, fill it, pay. Refilling is easier than it looks.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Our refill wall holds forty products, from oats and lentils to olive oil and washing-up liquid. If you have never used a refill shop, here is how it works.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Bring any clean container, or borrow one of ours.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Weigh it empty at the counter and we write the weight on the lid.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Fill it with as much or as little as you like.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pay only for what is inside.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:pantry-jars}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:pantry-jars}}\" alt=\"Refill jars with paper labels\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:pantry-jars}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Buying exactly the amount you need cuts food waste as well as packaging. A spoonful of a new spice costs a few cents, so experimenting is easy.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not sure where to start? Our staff are always happy to help with your first fill, and the shelf labels list the price per hundred grams.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "shop-news"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "zero-waste"
   ],
   "featured_media": "pantry-jars",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "meet-ilse-the-baker",
   "title": "Meet Ilse, our night-owl baker",
   "date": "2026-09-24T07:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Thirty-hour sourdough, stone-ground flour and a bakery that works while the village sleeps.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have bought a loaf from us before nine in the morning, it was probably still warm. Ilse starts work at midnight in a small bakery behind the mill.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why so slow?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Her dough ferments for thirty hours. The long rise develops flavour, gives the bread its open crumb and makes it easier to digest. It also means it keeps for days.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:bread}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:bread}}\" alt=\"Two sourdough loaves\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:bread}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Country sourdough and the seeded rye.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Flour, water, salt and time. Mostly time.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Ilse</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Look out for her cardamom buns on Saturdays. They sell out by ten.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "producers"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "bread"
   ],
   "featured_media": "baker-ilse",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Tom",
     "email": "tom@example.com",
     "content": "Those cardamom buns are worth getting up early for.",
     "date": "2026-09-26T09:55:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "strawberry-jam-small-batch",
   "title": "Small-batch strawberry jam",
   "date": "2026-09-08T15:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Three jars, one pan and no special kit. Late-season berries make the best jam.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The last strawberries of the season are often too soft for the shelves but perfect for jam. A small batch takes under an hour and fills three jars.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ingredients</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>1 kg strawberries, hulled</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>750 g sugar</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Juice of one lemon</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Method</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Crush the berries lightly with the sugar and leave them for an hour. Add the lemon juice, bring to a rolling boil and cook for about eight minutes, until a drop wrinkles on a cold plate.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Pour into warm, clean jars and seal at once. Bring the empty jars back to the shop and we will refill them next year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "recipes"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "preserving",
    "box-recipes"
   ],
   "featured_media": "strawberries",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "saturday-market-returns",
   "title": "The Saturday market is back on the green",
   "date": "2026-08-26T08:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Twelve stalls, a coffee cart and a children's seed-planting table every Saturday from September.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>After the summer break, the farmers' market returns to the village green every Saturday from 8 am to 1 pm.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who is coming</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Riverside Growers with salad and squash</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Millbrook End Orchard with apples and juice</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Hilltop Hens with eggs and chickens</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A cheese maker, a mushroom farm and a flower grower</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Bring a bag, bring the children, and stay for coffee. The shop stays open as usual for everything else.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every week one stall offers a cooking demonstration using whatever is in season, with free tastings until the pan is empty.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "shop-news"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "events"
   ],
   "featured_media": "hero-crate",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "five-salads-from-one-box",
   "title": "Five salads from one small box",
   "date": "2026-08-11T12:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Lunch for a week from a $24 box, with no sad leaves at the bottom of the fridge.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Salad leaves wilt fast, so plan the week the day the box arrives. These five ideas use everything up by Thursday.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Monday</strong>: tomato, white bean and basil</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Tuesday</strong>: grated carrot and apple with toasted seeds</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Wednesday</strong>: kale massaged with lemon and parmesan</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Thursday</strong>: roast beetroot with goat's cheese</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Friday</strong>: whatever is left, on toast</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Wash leaves as soon as you get home, wrap them in a damp tea towel and keep them in the fridge drawer. They will stay crisp for four or five days.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A simple dressing in a jar makes all the difference: three parts oil, one part vinegar, a spoon of mustard and a pinch of salt. It keeps for a week.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "recipes"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "vegetarian",
    "box-recipes"
   ],
   "featured_media": "salad",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  }
 ],
